        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Concrete is a sponge with a hard surface. An oil spill that looks like a puddle for the first ten minutes is actually migrating downward into a network of capillary pores, and within a day most of it is below the surface where no amount of scrubbing reaches. That single fact explains why degreaser and a stiff brush disappoint so often, and it points to the methods that do work: absorption while the oil is fresh, and poultice extraction once it is not.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Oil Stains Are So Hard to Remove -->
        <div id="why-oil-stains-stick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Oil Stains Are So Hard to Remove
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even a well-finished slab is roughly 10-15% air by volume, laced with capillary pores far too small to see. Oil, being low-viscosity and non-polar, wicks into that network readily — and the warmer the slab, the faster it travels. A summer spill on a hot driveway penetrates deeper in an hour than the same spill on a cold slab does in a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            That geometry defines the whole problem. Surface cleaning removes the oil you can see and leaves the oil that actually causes the stain. Within a few days the remaining subsurface oil wicks back up and the stain reappears, which is the frustrating cycle most homeowners recognize. Any method that genuinely works has to either get the oil before it penetrates, or reverse the direction of travel and pull it back out.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical air void content of concrete</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Window where absorbent powder works best</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-48 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Poultice dwell time for old stains</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">2-4x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Repeat cycles a deep stain usually needs</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Three Mechanisms That Actually Work</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Absorption</strong> uses a dry powder to soak up liquid oil before it penetrates. Only useful while the spill is still wet, but hugely effective in that window.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Poultice extraction</strong> applies a wet absorbent paste that soaks into the concrete, dissolves the oil, and then — as it dries — pulls the dissolved oil back up into itself by capillary action. This is the only method that removes oil from below the surface, and it is why poultices outperform everything else on old stains.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Biological digestion</strong> uses enzyme or microbial cleaners whose organisms consume hydrocarbons over days or weeks. Slow, but it reaches oil at depths a poultice cannot and leaves nothing behind to rinse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Fresh Spills: The First 30 Minutes -->
        <div id="fresh-spills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Fresh Spills: The First 30 Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the oil is still wet, you have the easiest job you will ever have with it, and speed matters more than product choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Blot, Never Wipe</h3>
          <p>
            Lay paper towels or rags on the spill and press. Wiping spreads the spill across more surface area and drives oil into the pores under pressure — you turn a small deep stain into a large one. Lift and replace towels until they stop coming up saturated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Cover With a Dry Absorbent</h3>
          <p>
            Pour a generous layer of dry absorbent over the whole spill — enough to fully cover, not a dusting. Options in rough order of effectiveness: clay-based oil absorbent (the granular product sold at auto parts stores), unscented clumping cat litter crushed fine, cornstarch, baking soda, or sawdust. Grind granular products underfoot or with a brick to break them down; finer particles have far more surface area and absorb much faster.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Sit</h3>
          <p>
            Give it a minimum of 30 minutes, and overnight is better for anything more than a few tablespoons. There is no downside to leaving it longer. Sweep up and dispose of the used absorbent in the trash — never rinse it into a storm drain, which is both illegal in most jurisdictions and genuinely harmful.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Degrease the Remaining Shadow</h3>
          <p>
            Whatever gray shadow remains is oil that already entered the surface layer. Apply a concrete degreaser or a strong dish soap solution, scrub with a stiff nylon brush, let it dwell 10-15 minutes without drying out, and rinse with hot water. On a fresh spill handled this way, this usually finishes the job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Do Not Reach for the Pressure Washer First</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Pressure washing a fresh oil spill drives oil deeper into the concrete and sprays it across a much wider area — turning one small stain into a large faint one that is harder to treat. Absorb first, degrease second, and only pressure wash at the end to remove loosened residue.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: Old, Set-In Stains: The Poultice Method -->
        <div id="old-stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Old, Set-In Stains: The Poultice Method
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a stain that is weeks, months, or years old, absorption is useless — the oil is already deep in the slab. The poultice is the method that works, and it is the single most effective technique in this entire guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How a Poultice Works</h3>
          <p>
            You mix an absorbent powder with a solvent into a paste roughly the consistency of peanut butter, spread it thickly over the stain, and seal it under plastic so it stays wet. The solvent soaks into the concrete and dissolves the trapped oil. Then you remove the plastic and let the poultice dry — and as it dries, capillary action pulls the dissolved oil up out of the concrete and into the drying paste. When you sweep it away, the oil goes with it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step-by-Step Poultice</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Choose your absorbent.</strong> Diatomaceous earth is the best performer. Fine clay absorbent, talc, or hydrated lime also work. Flour and cornstarch work in a pinch but are messier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Choose your solvent.</strong> Acetone or lacquer thinner for petroleum oils; mineral spirits for a gentler option; xylene for heavy grease. Work outdoors, wear nitrile gloves and eye protection, and keep all sources of ignition well away.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Mix to a thick paste</strong> — it should hold its shape on a putty knife without running.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. Apply half an inch thick,</strong> extending an inch or two past the visible stain edge, since the oil below is always wider than what you can see.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. Cover with plastic sheeting</strong> taped at the edges and leave for 24 hours. This keeps the solvent working rather than evaporating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>6. Remove the plastic and let it dry completely,</strong> usually another 24 hours. This drying phase is where extraction actually happens — do not rush it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>7. Scrape and sweep up the dried poultice,</strong> then rinse the area.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>8. Repeat.</strong> Deep stains typically need two to four cycles. Each pass visibly lightens the stain; when a cycle produces no further improvement, you have reached what is recoverable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Commercial Poultice Products</h3>
          <p>
            Pre-mixed oil stain removers using the same principle are widely available and remove the solvent-handling risk. They are more expensive per application than mixing your own but genuinely easier and safer, and the good ones perform comparably. If you are not comfortable handling acetone or xylene, this is the right route.
          </p>
        </div>

