          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Enzyme Cleaners Deserve More Attention</h3>
          <p>
            Microbial cleaners contain bacteria cultures that metabolize hydrocarbons, converting oil into carbon dioxide and water. They work slowly — days to weeks, kept damp — but they reach oil at depths that no poultice can pull from, and they leave nothing behind. For a driveway with years of accumulated drips, applying a microbial cleaner and keeping it damp for a couple of weeks often produces better results than any single aggressive treatment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Acid Is the Wrong Tool</h3>
          <p>
            Muriatic acid dissolves cement paste. It does not dissolve oil. What it actually does is etch away the top layer of concrete, which can make a stain look lighter by removing the surface, while permanently roughening the slab, exposing aggregate, and leaving it more absorbent and more prone to staining next time. Skip it.
          </p>
        </div>

