        <!-- SECTION 6: How Clean Can Concrete Actually Get? -->
        <div id="how-clean-is-clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Clean Can Concrete Actually Get?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part most guides avoid, and it matters for deciding when to stop spending weekends on a stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fresh spills</strong> handled within the first hour usually come out to invisible. This is the strongest argument for keeping absorbent in the garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stains a few weeks old</strong> typically clean up 80-95% with two or three poultice cycles, leaving a faint shadow that fades further over months of weathering and UV exposure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stains years old,</strong> particularly on unsealed concrete under a vehicle, generally reach 50-80% improvement. Oil that has been in the slab for years has migrated deep enough that some of it is simply not recoverable. Repeated poultices plus a microbial treatment gets the most out of it, but expect a visible difference rather than a perfect slab.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Know When to Switch Strategies</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If two full poultice cycles produce no further lightening, the remaining oil is beyond extraction. At that point your options are to accept it, resurface with a concrete overlay, or apply an opaque coating like a garage floor epoxy — but note that oil-contaminated concrete is a poor bonding surface, so it must be degreased thoroughly and often mechanically ground before any coating will adhere.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Note on Coatings Over Old Oil</h3>
          <p>
            Epoxy and other coatings fail on oil-contaminated concrete — the oil prevents adhesion and the coating delaminates in sheets within a year or two. If a coating is your plan, budget for thorough degreasing plus mechanical grinding or shot blasting to remove the contaminated surface layer, and expect the installer to test adhesion before proceeding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Preventing the Next Oil Stain -->
        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Preventing the Next Oil Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything above is remedial. The prevention side is cheap and takes an afternoon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seal the Concrete</h3>
          <p>
            A penetrating siloxane or silane sealer soaks into the surface and lines the capillary pores, dramatically slowing oil penetration and buying you the time to absorb a spill. It does not change the appearance of the slab and typically lasts 5-10 years. This is the single highest-value preventive step for a driveway or garage floor, and it costs a fraction of what stain removal takes in weekends.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Absorbent Where the Spill Will Happen</h3>
          <p>
            A bag of clay oil absorbent in the corner of the garage is a few dollars and turns a permanent stain into a thirty-minute cleanup. The determining factor in oil stain outcomes is almost always how quickly something got poured on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use a Containment Mat Under a Leaking Vehicle</h3>
          <p>
            If a vehicle drips persistently, an absorbent parking mat or even a sheet of cardboard swapped monthly costs almost nothing and prevents the years-deep contamination that never fully comes out. This is the difference between a stain you can remove and one you cannot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Treat New Stains Immediately</h3>
          <p>
            The cost of dealing with an oil stain roughly multiplies with every week it sits. A spill treated the same day takes thirty minutes and a bag of absorbent; the same spill in a year takes multiple poultice cycles across several weekends and still leaves a shadow.
          </p>
        </div>

