              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you get old oil stains out of concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a poultice. Mix an absorbent like diatomaceous earth with a solvent such as acetone or mineral spirits into a peanut-butter-thick paste, spread it half an inch thick over the stain, cover with taped plastic for 24 hours, then uncover and let it dry completely. The drying phase pulls dissolved oil up out of the concrete. Deep stains usually need two to four cycles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Dawn dish soap remove oil from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It works better than expected on fresh, light spills because it is a strong surfactant that emulsifies oil. Scrub it in with hot water, let it dwell 10-15 minutes, and rinse. It does nothing for oil that has already soaked into the slab, which is most stains older than a day or two.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a pressure washer remove oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. Pressure washing removes surface oil and drives the rest deeper while spreading it across a wider area. Use it as a final rinse after a degreaser or poultice has done the actual work, never as the first step on a fresh spill.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you get oil stains out of concrete completely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on age. A spill treated within the first hour usually comes out to invisible. A stain a few weeks old typically cleans up 80-95% with two or three poultice cycles. A years-old stain generally reaches 50-80% improvement, because some of the oil has migrated too deep to extract.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does WD-40 or Coca-Cola remove oil from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Coca-Cola is mild phosphoric acid with no affinity for oil and no extraction mechanism — it is a persistent myth. WD-40 is itself a petroleum product, so you are adding oil to an oil stain. Skip both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best product to remove oil from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For fresh spills, clay absorbent followed by Oil Eater degreaser. For stains a few weeks to months old, a poultice — Pour-N-Restore if you want it pre-mixed, diatomaceous earth plus acetone if you are comfortable with solvents. For years-old stains, a microbial cleaner like Terminator-HSD reaches deepest, though it takes weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you keep oil from staining concrete in the first place?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Apply a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer, which lines the capillary pores and buys you time to absorb a spill before it soaks in. Keep a bag of clay oil absorbent in the garage, and put an absorbent mat or cardboard under any vehicle that drips persistently. Sealing is the highest-value step and typically lasts 5-10 years.
              </p>
            </div>
