        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Mistakes That Make Oil Stains Worse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Several common first reactions actively make the stain harder to remove. These are the ones worth unlearning.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wiping Instead of Blotting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wiping a fresh spill spreads it over more surface area and presses oil into the pores. Lay towels flat, press down, lift, and replace. A small deep stain is far easier to remove than a large faint one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-pressure water drives oil deeper into the slab and atomizes it across a wider area. Absorb first, degrease second, and use the pressure washer only at the end to rinse away what has been loosened.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Muriatic Acid</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid dissolves cement paste, not oil. It etches away the surface, which can make a stain look lighter while permanently roughening the concrete, exposing aggregate, and leaving the slab more absorbent and more prone to staining next time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Giving Up After One Poultice Cycle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Poultices work incrementally, and a deep stain typically needs two to four passes. Each cycle visibly lightens the stain. Stop when a full cycle produces no further improvement — that is the point where the remaining oil is genuinely beyond extraction, not the point after the first attempt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Oil-Contaminated Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy and other coatings will not bond to oil-soaked concrete and delaminate in sheets within a year or two. If a coating is the plan, the slab must be degreased thoroughly and usually mechanically ground to remove the contaminated surface layer first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

